  [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m reads and writes, and the accessors. Nothing outside it ever touches [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m directly — that's
  the property that makes the persistence decision cheap to revisit and makes B a later extraction rather than a
  rewrite.

  Its public surface is three exports:

  - [38;5;153masync login(username, password)[39m — authenticates, persists the returned id, returns the result object.
  - [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m — returns the current id, or [38;5;153mnull[39m if nobody is logged in. This is the one function your future forms
    import.
  - [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m — removes it.

  [38;5;153mclearSession[39m is the one speculative export: there's no logout UI today. It's three lines and a session store with no
  way to clear it is a trap, but say the word and I'll cut it under YAGNI.

  [1mThe stub, shaped like the real thing.[0m [38;5;153mlogin[39m is [38;5;153masync[39m and returns [38;5;153m{ success, userId, username }[39m — the shape a real [38;5;153mPOST
[39m  response would have. The stub body resolves immediately with a fake id of the form [38;5;153mstub-<username>[39m. Two deliberate
  choices there: it's [3mdeterministic[0m, so the same account yields the same id across logins the way a real backend would
  (a fresh random UUID per login would quietly model the wrong thing); and it's [3mobviously fake[0m, so a stub id appearing
  in a log or a bug report can't be mistaken for production data. Swapping in the real backend later replaces one
  function body and touches no caller.

  [1mData flow.[0m Submit → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m (unchanged, stays in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m) → [38;5;153mawait login(...)[39m → [38;5;153mauth.js[39m persists the id → later
  forms call [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m. [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m moves out of [38;5;153mapp.js[39m into [38;5;153mauth.js[39m, since it belongs with the code that will
  eventually use it.

  [1mThe ripple you should know about.[0m Making [38;5;153mlogin[39m async makes its caller async: the submit handler becomes [38;5;153masync (e) => [39m
  [38;5;153m{...}[39m. That's contained today because there's exactly one caller, which is precisely why doing it now is cheap and
  doing it after five forms exist is not.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m keeps [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m and the DOM wiring and loses the login logic. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes to [38;5;153m<script type="module" [39m
  [38;5;153msrc="app.js">[39m.

  Does section 1 look right?

